ntfs-3g (ntfs-macos) patch 0012: security descriptor lengths from $SII and $SDH are bounded before they allocate

Rationale (T-M3-25, ADR-026, finding F1, GHSA-r4cv-86xq-37qj, tests/fuzz/TRIAGE.md): in NTFS-3G 2026.9.28,
libntfs-3g/security.c retrievesecurityattr() (lines 1823 to 1832) takes datasize from the $SII index entry
(a 32-bit on-disk value), computes size = datasize - sizeof(SECURITY_DESCRIPTOR_HEADER) and calls
ntfs_malloc(size) before it knows that $SDS holds that many bytes. With datasize < 20 the size_t
subtraction wraps (valgrind: "Argument 'size' of function malloc has a fishy (possibly negative) value:
-19"); with a large datasize the request is up to 4 GiB (libFuzzer: malloc(4294967147)), freed when the
following read fails. It is reached through getsecurityattr() (security.c:1885) from every permission check
and from getxattr system.ntfs_acl. Resource use, not a memory fault.
Change (libntfs-3g/security.c):
  - New static helper sds_record_within(ni, offs, length): opens the $SDS data attribute of $Secure
    (ntfs_attr_open with STREAM_SDS) and returns TRUE only when offs <= data_size and
    length <= data_size - offs (unsigned arithmetic, no overflow), and the record does not cross a block
    of $SDS: (offs & (ALIGN_SDS_BLOCK - 1)) + length <= ALIGN_SDS_BLOCK (256 KiB). The block rule is there
    because data_size is itself on-disk data: a crafted huge data_size with a small initialized_size would
    still allow an allocation of up to 4 GiB. entersecurityattr() never writes a record across a block
    (security.c lines 771 to 781) and Windows uses the same layout. Failure to open the stream or a
    negative size is "reject". Placed before setsecurityattr() so that both users see it.
  - retrievesecurityattr(): datasize is read once; the allocation happens only when
    datasize >= sizeof(SECURITY_DESCRIPTOR_HEADER) and the whole record (offset of the header and datasize,
    that is offs + size in the terms of the old code) lies within $SDS. Otherwise securattr stays NULL and
    the existing error path runs unchanged (log "Failed to retrieve a security descriptor", errno EIO).
  - setsecurityattr() (write path, line 879 to 892): see the sibling check below.
Sibling uses of datasize in security.c, checked:
  - entersecurityattr() (lines 707 and 734, pristine numbers): size = le32_to_cpu(psii->datasize) is
    a 32-bit value used only to compute the next free offset in $SDS (offs += gap + size); nothing is
    allocated or read from it. Not changed.
  - setsecurityattr() (line 879): size = datasize - header was an unguarded size_t subtraction followed by
    ntfs_malloc(size) for a record whose $SDH hash matches (read-write mounts only, to compare a new
    descriptor with the stored one). Same defect: fixed. The subtraction is made only when datasize is
    larger than the header (size is 0 otherwise, which the existing "size > 0" test already skips). A
    matching-hash record is compared only when its size equals attrsz (the caller's buffer holds attrsz
    bytes, so memcmp(oldattr, attr, size) over-read it when the on-disk size was larger) and it passes
    sds_record_within(); any other record is treated like a hash collision (the next entry is tried,
    nothing is allocated or compared).
Scope: library-wide and cross-platform (no __APPLE__ guard). Only adds rejections of malformed on-disk
data; a well-formed record lies within $SDS (the data is written to $SDS before the indexes) and takes the
same path as before. One extra ntfs_attr_open of $SDS happens per descriptor read from disk (a cache miss
in the security cache) and per matching-hash record on the write path.
Upstream status: reported privately 2026-11-16 as GHSA-r4cv-86xq-37qj; carried until a fixed NTFS-3G release
is pinned, then removed (ADR-026).
Test: ctest fuzz.findings.F1 replays tests/fuzz/image/findings/F1-getsecurityattr-alloc.bin through
fuzz_replay_mountwalk (security reads on, ASan allocation limit set in the test), registered only for an ASan
build. Verified in Environment A: on the unpatched library it fails (allocation-size-too-big, request
0xffffffffffffffed, the wrapped size) and passes with the patch, in clang and gcc builds at -O0 and -O1, with
and without UBSan. The image holds the datasize < header case; the large-datasize case and the block rule
have no reproducer image and rest on the logic above. Standalone image under valgrind (repro_secdesc): "fishy"
malloc of -19 unpatched, none patched. The write-path changes (setsecurityattr) are exercised by the
I-* cases that create files and set permissions in Environment A (48 pass with the patched build); no case
makes a hash match with a differing size.

--- a/libntfs-3g/security.c
+++ b/libntfs-3g/security.c
@@ -808,6 +808,41 @@
 }
 
 /*
+ *	Check that a record of $Secure:$SDS lies within the stream
+ *
+ *	The offset and the length come from an index entry of $SII or $SDH,
+ *	which is on-disk data and cannot be trusted before it is compared
+ *	with the size of the stream (ntfs-macos 0012).
+ *
+ *	Returns TRUE if [offs, offs + length) is within the data of $SDS
+ *		FALSE if not, or if the size of $SDS cannot be determined
+ */
+
+static BOOL sds_record_within(ntfs_inode *ni, u64 offs, u32 length)
+{
+	ntfs_attr *na;
+	BOOL ok;
+
+	ok = FALSE;
+	na = ntfs_attr_open(ni, AT_DATA, STREAM_SDS, 4);
+	if (na) {
+		/*
+		 * data_size is on-disk data too, so also require that the
+		 * record does not cross a block of $SDS : entersecurityattr()
+		 * never writes one across a block (a record is at most as
+		 * long as the block), and so does Windows
+		 */
+		ok = (na->data_size >= 0)
+		    && (offs <= (u64)na->data_size)
+		    && ((u64)length <= (u64)na->data_size - offs)
+		    && ((offs & (ALIGN_SDS_BLOCK - 1)) + length
+							<= ALIGN_SDS_BLOCK);
+		ntfs_attr_close(na);
+	}
+	return (ok);
+}
+
